00:33:32.470 --> 00:33:56.840
<v Miki Meek>This is what victory looks like, taking the trash out every day. He recently spotted one of the hoaxers who's been after him. Turns out, the person lives nearby. Lenny's about to have to move again. This will be the eighth time.
<v Miki Meek>这就是胜利的样子，每天倒垃圾。他最近发现了追逐他的骗子之一。结果那个人就住在附近。Lenny又要搬家了。这将是第八次。

00:33:56.840 --> 00:34:11.600
<v Ira Glass>Miki Meek is one of the producers of our show. Coming up, how Alex Jones became Alex Jones, or not. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>Miki Meek是我们节目的制作人之一。接下来，Alex Jones是如何成为Alex Jones的，或者没有成为。请稍候，来自芝加哥公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:32.340
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, Beware the Jabberwock. We've arrived at Act 2 of our program. Act 2. Alex in Wonderland. So one of the occasional contributors to our program, Jon Ronson, actually got to know Alex Jones personally years ago, long before Alex Jones became the most notorious conspiracy theorist in the country.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是Ira Glass。今天的节目，当心Jabberwock。我们已经到达了节目的第二幕。第二幕。仙境中的Alex。所以我们节目的偶尔贡献者Jon Ronson，实际上多年前就私下认识了Alex Jones，远在Alex Jones成为该国最臭名昭著的阴谋论者之前。

00:34:32.340 --> 00:34:47.760
<v Ira Glass>They met doing a story where, together, they snuck into a summertime enclave of the rich and powerful called Bohemian Grove. They each did their own stories about it. Jon thought it was pretty harmless, maybe a little silly. Alex Jones thought it was much more sinister.
<v Ira Glass>他们见面做了一个故事，一起溜进了一个叫波西米亚丛林的有钱有势者的夏季飞地。他们每个人都做了关于它的故事。Jon认为这相当无害，也许有点傻。Alex Jones认为它更邪恶。

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:57.150
<v Ira Glass>Now and then over the years, they would touch base. Then in 2016, Jon met somebody in Alex's orbit. And that is where this story begins.
<v Ira Glass>多年来偶尔，他们会保持联系。然后在2016年，Jon遇到了Alex身边的人。而这就是这个故事开始的地方。

00:34:57.150 --> 00:35:25.080
<v Jon  Ronson>The man's name was Josh Owens, and in October 2016 he started sending me cryptic texts about his boss. "A lot of times, I'm conflicted with what I'm seeing. Anyway, I won't get into that." His boss was Alex Jones. Josh worked for him as a cameraman and a field producer. We exchanged more texts, and then we arranged to meet in a hotel lobby in Washington, DC.
<v Jon  Ronson>那个人的名字是Josh Owens，2016年10月他开始给我发送关于他老板的神秘短信。“很多时候，我对所看到的感到矛盾。不管怎样，我不想深入谈论那个。”他的老板是Alex Jones。Josh作为摄像师和现场制作人为他工作。我们交换了更多短信，然后安排在华盛顿特区的一家酒店大堂见面。

00:35:25.080 --> 00:35:53.340
<v Jon  Ronson>And here he was, walking in from the street, and waving shyly. I was struck by how nervous he looked. He had traveled here to tell me secrets about Alex. And the reason he chose me was because I have known Alex Jones for so long. When I first met and wrote about him 20 years ago, he broadcast Infowars down an ISDN line from a child's bedroom in his house, which was covered in choo choo train wallpaper.
<v Jon  Ronson>他就在这里，从街上走进来，羞怯地挥手。他看起来有多紧张让我印象深刻。他来这里是为了告诉我关于Alex的秘密。而他选择我的原因是，我认识Alex Jones太久了。当我20年前第一次见面并写关于他时，他通过ISDN线路从他房子里的儿童卧室广播InfoWars，那里贴满了火车墙纸。

00:35:53.340 --> 00:36:02.760
<v Jon  Ronson>Back then, the whole operation was him, his producer Mike, and his webmaster executive producer girlfriend Kelly, also known as Violet.
<v Jon  Ronson>当时，整个行动是他，他的制作人Mike，还有他的网站管理员执行制作人女友Kelly，也被称为Violet。

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:22.620
<v Violet>Well, Alex at 26-years-old is now syndicated to over 40 commercial markets. So he's huge, not to mention that he's on short wave and the internet. He's been number one on AOL numerous times. Alex is a sensation, because he's young, he's vibrant, and so many people are getting involved with this movement through what Alex does.
<v Violet>嗯，26岁的Alex现在已联合到40多个商业市场。所以他很大，更不用说他在短波和互联网上了。他多次在AOL上排名第一。Alex是个轰动，因为他年轻、充满活力，而且许多人通过Alex所做的事情参与到了这个运动中。

00:36:22.620 --> 00:36:43.550
<v Jon  Ronson>This is tape I recorded back in 1999. Violet would eventually become Alex's wife, and then years later, his ex-wife in an extremely acrimonious divorce and custody battle, during which Alex would at one point stare at her from the dock and tell the court, "she doesn't have any good qualities."
<v Jon  Ronson>这是我1999年录下的磁带。Violet最终成为了Alex的妻子，然后多年后，在他的极度痛苦的离婚和监护权争夺战中，Alex一度从被告席盯着她并告诉法庭，“她没有任何优点。”

00:36:43.550 --> 00:36:57.330
<v Alex Jones>You're beautiful, my love. Isn't my girlfriend, Violet Nichols, beautiful and smart? Look at her. Look at her. She is just incredibly smart. That's the webmaster of infowars.com.
<v Alex Jones>你很美，我的爱。我的女朋友Violet Nichols不是很漂亮又聪明吗？看她。看她。她简直聪明得不可思议。那是infowars.com的网站管理员。

00:36:57.330 --> 00:37:30.330
<v Jon  Ronson>I followed Alex around for weeks that year. He was different back then, an eccentric fledgling conspiracy broadcaster. I can't remember him attacking anyone but the powerful in those days. He wasn't going after victims of school shootings as crisis actors. He didn't assert, as he did in 2017, that the yogurt maker Chobani was, quote, importing migrant rapists who were spreading tuberculosis. He later apologized to Chobani in the face of a lawsuit.
<v Jon  Ronson>那一年我跟着Alex跑了几个星期。那时的他不同，一个古怪的羽翼未丰的阴谋广播员。我不记得他在那些日子里攻击过任何人，除了强者。他没有去追求校园枪击案的受害者作为危机演员。他没有主张，正如他在2017年所做的那样，酸奶机Chobani是，引用道，进口正在传播结核病的移民强奸犯。面对诉讼，他后来向Chobani道歉。

00:37:30.330 --> 00:38:01.890
<v Jon  Ronson>He has money now, a workforce of something like 40 employees, and a fan in President Trump, who has repeated a bunch of theories championed by Alex that Barack Obama is a Muslim who wears a ring with an Arabic inscription. That Ted Cruz's father was linked to the JFK assassination, that Obama and Hillary co-founded ISIS. Alex is playing a role in propagating the misinformation and confusion that's permeating America.
<v Jon  Ronson>他现在有钱了，有40名员工的劳动力，还有特朗普总统这个粉丝，他重复了Alex倡导的一堆理论，即巴拉克·奥巴马是一个戴着有阿拉伯铭文戒指的穆斯林。泰德·克鲁兹的父亲与肯尼迪遇刺有关，奥巴马和希拉里共同创立了ISIS。Alex正在扮演一个角色，传播充斥美国的虚假信息和混乱。

00:38:01.890 --> 00:38:20.580
<v Alex Jones>With Hillary, she is an abject, psychopathic demon from hell that as soon as she gets into power, is gonna try to destroy the planet. Folks, let me just tell you something-- there are dozens of videos and photos of Obama having flies land on him indoors at all times of year, and he'll be next to 100 people, and no one has flies on 'em.
<v Alex Jones>关于希拉里，她是一个来自地狱的卑鄙、精神病恶魔，一旦她掌权，就会试图摧毁地球。伙计们，让我告诉你们一些事——有几十个视频和照片显示奥巴马全年都在室内有苍蝇落在身上，他旁边有100个人，没有人有苍蝇。

00:38:20.580 --> 00:38:37.300
<v Alex Jones>Hillary, reportedly-- I mean, I was told by people around her that they think she's demon-possessed, OK? You can't wash that evil off, man. I'm told there's a rotten smell around Hillary. I'm not kidding. People say Obama and Hillary both smell like sulfur. They smell like hell.
<v Alex Jones>希拉里，据报道——我的意思是，有人告诉我说他们认为她是恶魔附身，好吗？你洗不掉那份邪恶，伙计。有人告诉我奥巴马和希拉里闻起来都有硫磺味。他们闻起来像地狱。

00:38:37.300 --> 00:38:40.520
<v Jon  Ronson>Anyway, back to my meeting with Josh.
<v Jon  Ronson>不管怎样，回到我和Josh的会面。

00:38:40.520 --> 00:38:43.390
<v Josh Owen>I was a big listener of the show.
<v Josh Owen>我曾经是节目的忠实听众。

00:38:43.390 --> 00:39:05.980
<v Jon  Ronson>He said working for Alex had been his dream job. He'd actually won an online reporter contest four years earlier to work for Infowars. He was a young and angry Alex Jones fan back then, committed to exposing the shadowy elites. But now he was disillusioned, because Alex had changed. And the final straw was the Muslims.
<v Jon  Ronson>他说为Alex工作一直是他的梦想工作。他实际上四年前赢得了一场在线记者比赛，从而为InfoWars工作。他当时是一个年轻而愤怒的Alex Jones粉丝，致力于揭露阴暗的精英。但现在他幻灭了，因为Alex变了。最后的稻草是穆斯林。

00:39:05.980 --> 00:39:21.640
<v Jon  Ronson>Alex had lately been broadcasting untrue stories about American Muslims running secret terrorist training camps in the Catskill Mountains of New York, and cheering the 9/11 attacks. Here he is talking to then-candidate Donald Trump.
<v Jon  Ronson>Alex最近一直在广播关于美国穆斯林在纽约卡茨基尔山脉运营秘密恐怖训练营，并为“9·11”袭击欢呼的不实故事。这是他在和当时的候选人唐纳德·特朗普谈话。

00:39:21.640 --> 00:39:30.580
<v Alex Jones>You are vindicated on the radical Muslims celebrating not just in New Jersey, but New York, Palestine, all over. What do you have to say? They're still attacking you.
<v Alex Jones>你被平反了，关于激进穆斯林不仅在新泽西州，而且在纽约、巴勒斯坦各地庆祝。你有什么要说的？他们还在攻击你。

00:39:30.580 --> 00:39:38.550
<v Donald Trump>Well, I took a lot of heat, and I was very strong on it. I just want to finish by saying your reputation is amazing. I will not let you down.
<v Donald Trump>嗯，我承受了很多压力，我对此非常强硬。我只想最后说，你的名声太棒了。我不会让你失望的。

00:39:38.550 --> 00:40:08.940
<v Jon  Ronson>And so Josh had found himself intimately involved in the business of demonizing Muslims. This wasn't why he'd signed up. These weren't powerful elites. They were regular people. Recently, Josh boarded a plane and sat next to a Muslim family. The little girl was laughing, her face pressed to the window. Josh looked at her, and he hated himself. And soon after, he began texting me.
<v Jon  Ronson>所以Josh发现自己深入参与了妖魔化穆斯林的业务。这不是他报名参加的原因。这些不是有权势的精英。他们是普通人。最近，Josh登上一架飞机，坐在一个穆斯林家庭旁边。小女孩在笑，脸压在窗户上。Josh看着她，他恨自己。不久之后，他开始给我发短信。

00:40:08.940 --> 00:40:25.460
<v Jon  Ronson>He wanted to make amends. He said he knew he should quit working for Alex, but it was hard. Alex paid him well. And although he had seen Alex scream at people in the office, and punch cabinets, and rip down blinds, he'd only ever been nice to Josh.
<v Jon  Ronson>他想弥补。他说他知道他应该辞去Alex的工作，但这很难。Alex付给他很高的报酬。尽管他曾见过Alex在办公室里对人尖叫，并打柜子，撕下百叶窗，但他对他一直很好。

00:40:25.460 --> 00:40:50.040
<v Jon  Ronson>As we continued to talk, I realized that Josh hadn't come to tell me any specific secret about Alex. He just wanted to vent his frustrations to somebody else who had known him a long time. But then he said, oh, here's a story. The thing Josh told me then would, if true, totally rewrite the story of how Alex Jones became Alex Jones.
<v Jon  Ronson>当我们继续交谈时，我意识到Josh并没有告诉我关于Alex的任何具体秘密。他只是想向另一个认识他很久的人发泄他的挫折感。但然后他说，哦，这里有个故事。Josh当时告诉我的那件事，如果属实，将彻底重写Alex Jones如何成为Alex Jones的故事。

00:40:50.040 --> 00:41:03.670
<v Jon  Ronson>First, you should know that Alex has a kind of origin story about what got him obsessed with rooting out conspiracies. He's told it a few times to the Rolling Stone journalist Alexander Zaitchik, and during monologues on his show.
<v Jon  Ronson>首先，你应该知道Alex有一个关于他沉迷于根除阴谋论的起源故事。他曾几次告诉《滚石》杂志记者Alexander Zaitchik，并在他的节目独白中说。

00:41:03.670 --> 00:41:13.550
<v Alex Jones>When I was, like, 16-years-old, and I'd be at parties, in would come a sheriff's department car, and they'd come in and sell bags of drugs to people.
<v Alex Jones>当我大约16岁的时候，我去参加聚会，警长部门的车会开进来，他们会进来卖毒品给人们。

00:41:13.550 --> 00:41:17.830
<v Jon  Ronson>This took place in the Dallas suburb of Rockwall, where Alex grew up.
<v Jon  Ronson>这发生在达拉斯郊区的Rockwall，Alex在那里长大。

00:41:17.830 --> 00:41:39.620
<v Alex Jones>And I went to school, and they were having an auditorium meeting about drug testing, or something. They had cops up there that I knew were drug dealers. And I stood up, and I said, "I was at a pool party. He was selling cocaine and ecstasy last week." They took me in an office, rammed my head in the wall, told me, "we're gonna kill you if you don't shut up." They said, "You're gonna move out of this town right now." That's why we moved out here to Austin, because of it.
<v Alex Jones>我去学校，他们正在礼堂开会讨论药物测试，或其他什么。他们有警察在上面，我知道他们是毒贩。我站起来说，“我在参加泳池派对。他上周在卖可卡因和摇头丸。”他们把我带进办公室，把我的头撞在墙上，告诉我，“如果你再不闭嘴，我们就杀了你。”他们说，“你现在就搬出这个城镇。”这就是为什么我们搬到Austin，因为这个。

00:41:39.620 --> 00:41:54.660
<v Jon  Ronson>Police corruption in Rockwall had opened Alex's eyes to the existence of powerful cabals, and exposing them would become his calling. And it all began at that school assembly. So that's Alex's origin story.
<v Jon  Ronson>Rockwall的警察腐败打开了Alex对有权势帮派存在的认识，揭露它们将成为他的使命。这一切都始于那次学校集会。所以这就是Alex的起源故事。

00:41:54.660 --> 00:42:13.220
<v Jon  Ronson>But according to Josh, it wasn't true. The true story, he said, was much more revealing. And it was also a big secret. Josh said, OK, I've heard this story a couple of times from childhood friends and family members.
<v Jon  Ronson>但据Josh说，那不是真的。他说，真实的故事更具启发性。而且这也是一个大秘密。Josh说，好的，我从儿时朋友和家人那里听过这个故事几次。

00:42:13.220 --> 00:42:39.110
<v Jon  Ronson>Very few people in the office know it, but when Jones was a teenager, he was a bully. No one liked him, because he was a bully. There was a group of kids that felt unsafe around him, and so they came up with a plan to trick him. They invited him out to a party in a barn. It was a trap, Josh said. They beat him within an inch of his life. That's why his family moved to Austin.
<v Jon  Ronson>办公室里很少有人知道它，但当Jones是青少年时，他是个恶霸。没人喜欢他，因为他是个恶霸。有一群孩子觉得在他身边不安全，所以他们想出了一个捉弄他的计划。他们邀请他去参加一个谷仓里的派对。Josh说，那是个陷阱。他们把他打得半死。这就是为什么他的家人搬到了Austin。

00:42:39.110 --> 00:42:57.740
<v Jon  Ronson>His whole family uprooted him and moved into a completely different city because of this crazy thing that happened to him. Josh said having those guys conspire against Alex by luring him to that party was the real reason he got obsessed with conspiracies. He had been the victim of one.
<v Jon  Ronson>他的全家因为这件发生在他身上的疯狂事情而连根拔起，搬到了一个完全不同的城市。Josh说让那些家伙通过引诱他去那个派对来密谋对付Alex，是他沉迷于阴谋的真正原因。他成了其中一个受害者。

00:42:57.740 --> 00:43:22.160
<v Jon  Ronson>Josh and I parted soon after that. I walked around, replaying our conversation and wondering if I should look into it further. There were now two Alex Jones origin stories. In Alex's version, he was a brave figure forced out of town for standing up to corrupt authority. But in Josh's, he was an outcast run out of town by fellow high school kids.
<v Jon  Ronson>Josh和我在那之后不久分手了。我四处走动，重温我们的谈话，想知道我是否应该深入调查。现在有两个Alex Jones的起源故事。在Alex的版本中，他是一个勇敢的人物，因为反抗腐败权威而被逐出城镇。但在Josh的版本中，他是一个被高中同学赶出城镇的弃儿。

00:43:22.160 --> 00:43:46.910
<v Jon  Ronson>Had Alex still been the fringey Alex of the '90s, I would have left his childhood alone. But he had become an influential figure, spreading chaos. And according to Josh, being ambushed at that party was the moment that shaped him-- that made him who he was. And so I dug around some more.
<v Jon  Ronson>如果Alex仍然是90年代那个边缘的Alex，我会让他的童年独自留着。但他已经成为一个有影响力的人物，传播混乱。而根据Josh的说法，在那个派对上遭到伏击是他塑造自己的时刻——那让他成为了他。所以我又挖了一些。

00:43:46.910 --> 00:44:20.450
<v Jon  Ronson>It became really clear, really quickly that Alex's school assembly story did not stand up to scrutiny. My producer, Lina Misitzis, reached 10 people who were at the school at the time. And while it's true that the local sheriff had been convicted of stealing marijuana from the police evidence room with a plan to sell it, no one remembered anything like that assembly happening. Not the students, not the principal, not the teachers, not one of Alex's old football coaches, Randall Talley, who preferred to go by Coach Randy.
<v Jon  Ronson>很快就很清楚了，Alex的学校集会故事经不起推敲。我的制作人Lina Misitzis找到了10个当时在学校的人。虽然当地警长确实因为从警察证据室偷窃大麻并计划出售而被定罪是真的，但没有人记得发生过那种集会。不是学生，不是校长，不是老师，甚至不是Alex的老足球教练之一Randall Talley，他更喜欢被称为Randy教练。

00:44:20.450 --> 00:44:28.580
<v Lina Misitzis>Do you remember an assembly where Alex called out the cops in the assembly for being drug traffickers?
<v Lina Misitzis>你还记得Alex在集会上点名警察是毒贩的集会吗？

00:44:28.580 --> 00:44:30.410
<v Randall Talley>Well, I didn't know about that.
<v Randall Talley>嗯，我不知道那个。

00:44:30.410 --> 00:44:32.270
<v Lina Misitzis>Is that your first time hearing about it?
<v Lina Misitzis>这是你第一次听说它吗？

00:44:32.270 --> 00:44:33.260
<v Randall Talley>Yes.
<v Randall Talley>是的。

00:44:33.260 --> 00:44:36.200
<v Jon  Ronson>Here's a former classmate, Ryan Tipton.
<v Jon  Ronson>这是前同学Ryan Tipton。

00:44:36.200 --> 00:44:43.670
<v Ryan Tipton>No, not at all. That was probably DARE-- the DARE program. And I do not remember that at all.
<v Ryan Tipton>不，一点也不。那可能是DARE——DARE项目。我根本不记得那回事。

00:44:43.670 --> 00:44:46.540
<v Jon  Ronson>Here's an old school friend, Jacob Olsen.
<v Jon  Ronson>这是老朋友Jacob Olsen。

